    "definition": "(1) If the President is satisfied that a grave emergency exists whereby the security of India or any part thereof is threatened, whether by war, external aggression, or armed rebellion, the President may by Proclamation declare a state of Emergency. [The 44th Amendment replaced \"internal disturbance\" with \"armed rebellion.\"] (2) A Proclamation may be issued before the actual occurrence of war or aggression if the President is satisfied of imminent danger. (3) The President shall not issue a Proclamation unless the Cabinet (Council of Ministers) communicates its decision in writing. [Added by the 44th Amendment.] (4) Every Proclamation shall be laid before Parliament and shall cease to operate at the expiration of one month unless approved by both Houses by special majority (majority of total membership and two-thirds of members present and voting). (5) An approved Proclamation remains in force for six months, renewable indefinitely by further parliamentary approval. (7A) The President shall revoke a Proclamation if Lok Sabha passes a resolution disapproving it by simple majority.",
    "explanation": "Article 352 provides for the most drastic constitutional measure — a National Emergency that transforms the entire federal and rights framework. During a National Emergency: (a) the federal distribution of powers is suspended — Parliament can legislate on State List subjects (Article 250); (b) fundamental rights under Article 19 are automatically suspended (Article 358); (c) the President may suspend the right to move courts for enforcement of other fundamental rights (Article 359); and (d) the life of Lok Sabha may be extended beyond five years (Article 83).\n\nThree National Emergencies have been declared: (1) 1962 (Sino-Indian War); (2) 1971 (Indo-Pakistan War/Bangladesh Liberation); and (3) 1975-77 (Internal Emergency declared by Indira Gandhi — the most controversial).\n\nThe 1975 Emergency was a watershed moment in Indian constitutional history. Prime Minister Indira Gandhi declared Emergency on grounds of \"internal disturbance\" after the Allahabad High Court set aside her election. During the Emergency, fundamental rights were suspended, political opponents were imprisoned without trial (under MISA), press censorship was imposed, and forced sterilization programs were conducted. The Supreme Court's controversial decision in ADM Jabalpur v. Shivkant Shukla (1976) held that even the right to life (Article 21) was suspended during Emergency — a decision later overruled in K.S. Puttaswamy v. Union of India (2017).\n\nThe 44th Amendment (1978) — enacted after the Emergency was lifted — introduced critical safeguards: (a) replaced \"internal disturbance\" with \"armed rebellion\" (higher threshold); (b) required written Cabinet recommendation; (c) required special majority parliamentary approval within one month; (d) made presidential satisfaction subject to judicial review (S.R. Bommai); (e) protected Article 20 and 21 from suspension even during Emergency.",
